# Commits that are (almost) entirely formatting. `git blame` should look
# through them to whoever actually wrote the line.
#
# Not applied automatically — git needs to be told once, per clone:
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# (GitHub's blame view honours this file without any configuration.)
#
# ── How to add one, and the trap that caught the first entry ──────────────
#
# This repo squash-merges every PR (PGR-005), so **one PR becomes exactly one
# commit on `main`**. Splitting a reformat into its own *commit* on a branch
# therefore achieves nothing: the squash merges it back together, and the
# branch SHA you were going to list here never lands on `main` at all.
#
# So a blame-ignorable reformat must be **its own PR**, containing nothing
# else. Then list the squashed commit's SHA (from `main`, after merging) here.
#
# Do not list a commit that mixes a reformat with real edits — hiding it
# hides the edits too. The one exception below is grandfathered and labelled.

# style: apply cargo fmt across the workspace — 58 files, +4,769/-1,217.
#
# GRANDFATHERED, and an honest caveat: this squash (#47) also carried ~33
# lines that are NOT formatting — the `cargo fmt --check` CI step in
# ci.yml, plus CLAUDE.md's gate list and roadmap item 10. Listing it hides
# their blame too. That is the mistake this file's guidance above now
# exists to prevent: the reformat and the gate should have been two PRs,
# not two commits in one PR. Kept anyway because ignoring ~33 lines of
# CI/doc blame is much cheaper than losing blame on 4,769 reformatted
# source lines, and `git log` still finds the CI change either way.
